    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.question",
        "pk": 12796,
        "fields": {
            "priority": 0,
            "description": "The horrific terrorist attacks on America have, rather incongruently, massively increased President Bush’s popularity — especially after his rousing State Of The Union address. His administration has lined up a series of countries on a terrorism hit-list. Article V of NATO is invoked by the White House, as fighter jets scream above the Atlantic. From Cuba to North Korea, they settle on the easiest fish to fry: Iraq. If you want to get involved in kicking out Saddam Hussein for good, you better decide now.",
            "likelihood": 1
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.question",
        "pk": 12801,
        "fields": {
            "priority": 0,
            "description": "Let’s shift perspectives, if only for a moment. You are Chancellor of the Exchequer, Gordon Brown. Today, you will unveil the 2002 Budget titled: “The strength to make long-term decisions: Investing in an enterprising, fairer Britain”. This <i>could</i> have been done in a more orderly manner, if it weren't for Tony stealing your policy on a show with Andrew Marr. Alas – however much it frustrated you – you will push on. The NHS is in need of a funding hike to meet the EU standard. To meet this: the main tax you will raise will be…",
            "likelihood": 1
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.question",
        "pk": 12806,
        "fields": {
            "priority": 0,
            "description": "Continuing as Chancellor for just a moment more, you have always had your own ambitions as Chancellor. From the shrinking of wealth inequality, to reshaping the social fabric of society. One thing that has always remained the same though is New Labour’s commitment to preserving – for the most part – the privatisation policy of the Thatcher government. Will you continue this line of political thought, of the public-private relationship, or will you seek a different path? What did Tony set out for you in your many gritting meetings?",
            "likelihood": 1
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.question",
        "pk": 12811,
        "fields": {
            "priority": 0,
            "description": "Now that you have decided to go through with supporting the United States’ initiative, a new daunting task lies ahead. Whilst you have compiled a decent enough justification for a pursuit of “disarming Iraq”, the scrutiny of the international community could be the deciding factor. Iraq has agreed to the presence of UN weapons inspectors under the passage of the first <i>Iraq UN Resolution</i>. Permanent Council members France and Russia look on with trepidation. But, both British and American intelligence already suspect that WMDs are still present in the nation. Salivating at the prospect of sinking into the Middle-Eastern dictatorship. How will you try to gain support for the war when your primary reasoning is already starting to look shaky?",
            "likelihood": 1
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.question",
        "pk": 15085,
        "fields": {
            "priority": 0,
            "description": "The UN Assembly has spoken, and it has delivered its definitive and bruising decision. There will not be a second resolution. France and Germany killed all momentum for the proposal with a motion of their own merely extending the deadline for the weapons inspectors, and the 'Swing Six' in the wider council have soundly rejected it too. The American military has lined up their ducks in a row, guns trained and fingers itching to pull. Kuwait has become a virtual country-sized American base. If they move forward, they're going to bring the country down. So, you have to make the case for it anyway. What will you say to a public already scrutinising the idea of a war with Iraq?",
            "likelihood": 1
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.question",
        "pk": 14047,
        "fields": {
            "priority": 0,
            "description": "The world outside our fair shores, thought to be gentle and peaceful, has been anything but that. Even still, you were elected to govern the United Kingdom – and by God – that is what you are going to do. Your mandate from 2001 still grants you a large majority in parliament, and you intend to use it. The only question is: What will you do with it?",
            "likelihood": 1
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.question",
        "pk": 12816,
        "fields": {
            "priority": 0,
            "description": "Ever since that fateful day in 2001, your government – and the agencies of MI5, MI6, and the CIA – have been diligent to gather and fabricate evidence for the United States’ plan to invade Iraq. Now – with the American Congress overwhelmingly passing the “Joint Resolution To Authorise The Use Of United States Armed Forces Against Iraq” – their ambitions have finally been realised. Soon, rocket flares shall begin to illuminate the Iraqi skies as a culmination of these subterfuges. The fateful decision falls to you.<p></p>The weight of an entire world rests upon your shoulders. The only thing stopping British troops from marching into Iraq is an anti-war vote in the House of Commons – it falls to you, whether you wish to shoot it down.",
            "likelihood": 1
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.question",
        "pk": 14814,
        "fields": {
            "priority": 0,
            "description": "The residence directly adjacent to yours is scheming – you're sure of it. No. 11's team of PR people and officials are doing everything in their ability to slow you down, to raise up Gordon and undermine <i>your</i> authority. It's a pestering and bedeviling ache in your mind. What could the Chancellor possibly pull next?<p></p>Yet now, he's right next to you. Right in front of your face. His jowls sag as his grumbling and scowls trickle out in a stream of harboured resentment. You need to sort something out with him – and now. Before anything more spills over.",
            "likelihood": 1
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.question",
        "pk": 12821,
        "fields": {
            "priority": 0,
            "description": "It appears your commitment to going through with the exaggerated evidence is starting to bite you in the back. A whistleblower has been reaching out to journalist Andrew Gilligan of the BBC, claiming that the dossiers of evidence to propel a war in Iraq were “sexed up” to get public opinion on your government’s side. These media attacks could be damaging, and chokingly drag down your approval ratings as Prime Minister. How will you move on this?",
            "likelihood": 1
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.question",
        "pk": 14048,
        "fields": {
            "priority": 0,
            "description": "The house of cards that Party Leader Iain Duncan Smith has tried to lay down, having been teetering since his election in 2001, has finally crashed down. After daring to challenge his MPs to a vote, his blasé was met with the full force and ruthlessness of the Conservative Party. He has lost his own confidence vote 90 to 75. Eight days on, the fate of the newly-created vacancy – consummated by the bowing out of David Davis, Ancram, and even Clarke – culminated in former Home Secretary Michael Howard being elected unopposed. Ahead of his first PMQs as Opposition Leader, where should we train our guns to nail him in the head?",
            "likelihood": 1
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.question",
        "pk": 12826,
        "fields": {
            "priority": 0,
            "description": "In 1998, your government passed up-front fees for students every year that they studied. This action had in essence done away with the consensus of education as a free right for the upcoming workforce. With universities breathing down your neck to keep their budgets in balance, you are now pushing forward the Higher Education Bill of 2004. This bill, if enacted, would replace the up-front fees system with a loan system. Your advisors press that given the right play, it will pass. Nevertheless, it is deeply controversial and could very well risk failing the vote on the second reading. How will you ensure that it passes?",
            "likelihood": 1
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.question",
        "pk": 12831,
        "fields": {
            "priority": 0,
            "description": "The air of Westminster reeks of distrust. The whistleblower, having been revealed to be Weapons Expert Dr David Kelly, appeared before a select committee to testify on the evidence surrounding your dossiers making the case for Iraq. Two days after he was set to appear before said committee, Dr. Kelly was found dead in a forest near his home. The public backlash forced you in response, to commission an inquiry led by Lord Hutton surrounding his death and the dossiers of evidence published. Today the inquiry has been published, and it is time to see the verdict of the report.",
            "likelihood": 1
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.question",
        "pk": 14051,
        "fields": {
            "priority": 0,
            "description": "The war-time advantage you once enjoyed as Prime Minister is finally starting to fray. You are beginning to be pressured by British Human Rights groups and MPs due to the government's sweeping powers under the Anti-Terrorism Crime and Security Act, which have allowed the detention of fourteen foreign terrorist suspects in the UK at what has been described as \"Britain's Guantanamo Bay\". How will you react?",
            "likelihood": 1
        }
